<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  macOS hardened-runtime entitlements.

  Without these the bundled app is killed on launch — SIGKILL, code
  signature invalid, CODESIGNING/"Invalid Page" — the moment wasmtime
  compiles a plugin component. Nothing in the crash report names
  wasmtime or plugins, so the symptom is "the app does not start" and
  the cause is four layers away.

  It only bites the bundled build. `cargo run` produces a linker-signed
  binary with no hardened runtime, so JIT is unrestricted there and
  every test and dev run passes. `tauri build` sets the runtime flag,
  and that flag is what turns JIT-compiled pages into a kill.

  Both keys are needed, and this was determined by testing rather than
  assumed:

  - `allow-jit` permits MAP_JIT mappings. On its own it is NOT enough:
    the app still died, because wasmtime does not use MAP_JIT — it maps
    pages and then mprotects them executable.
  - `allow-unsigned-executable-memory` is what permits that, and adding
    it is what made the app start.

  Both are kept. `allow-jit` costs nothing and is the entitlement a
  future wasmtime would use if it moved to MAP_JIT, which is the
  narrower and better-protected path of the two.

  What this gives up: executable pages in this process are no longer
  required to be signed. That is inherent to running a JIT at all, and
  is the trade the plugin system makes by executing WebAssembly. The
  sandbox around plugin code is wasmtime's — the memory limits, the
  epoch deadlines, the capability-gated imports — not the platform's
  page protection.

  Deliberately NOT included: `disable-executable-page-protection`,
  which switches the enforcement off process-wide rather than
  permitting the specific thing that needs permitting.

  ## Loading the bundled fbclient

  The hardened runtime also turns on *library validation*, which only
  loads libraries signed by the same Team ID as the process or by
  Apple. The bundled `libfbclient.dylib` is ad-hoc signed with no team,
  and so is this app, so dyld refuses it:

      code signature ... not valid for use in process:
      mapping process and mapped file (non-platform) have different
      Team IDs

  That surfaces as `profile_connect` failing for every profile using
  the native driver — which is all of them by default — with nothing in
  the message about signing.

  `disable-library-validation` is the entitlement for this, and the
  alternative is not better: signing the vendored Firebird libraries
  with our own identity would mean re-signing a third-party binary on
  every build to claim it is ours.

  Same caveat as above about where the trust boundary actually is. The
  fbclient we load is one we vendored into the bundle; it is not a
  library a plugin or a user can substitute.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
